WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
Cobra Command, take to the skies on a daring rescue mission!

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:26.600
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.540
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.920
And that's it.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.040
That's everybody. Because everyone else has abandoned us.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.660
Why have you forsaken me?

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.740
Not necessarily true.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.280
They'll probably be back in another episode, maybe even next episode.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:48.920
Who can say? But right now, you have the pleasure of listening to just me and Sean hash out the details of Cobra Command.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:55.440
It's always an intimate time if you listen to this with just a little bit of wine and candlelight.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.100
It's always a good time.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.440
Sean, I publish the episodes at eight in the morning on Fridays.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.260
Well, if you black out the windows and you start early, you can still do that.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.280
All right. You know, we're talking about a Data East game here.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:12.940
Do you look for that label at all?

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.680
Do you ever care about who made the game before you start playing these games on Nostalgia?

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:22.760
Yeah. And I would say that Data East is one of those companies that I prefer not make my games.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.000
Yeah. They don't have a great track record.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.160
Even I think I knew that even going into Nostalgia when we started doing this show.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.960
But it really has like stuck out as a name that like that.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.160
And I think just for some reason, Micronics comes to mind, too.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.640
It's just like a company I didn't like.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.400
I would say the same about both.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:49.240
Yeah. So when I see the logo for Data East, it's not welcoming.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.020
It doesn't make me think like I'm about to have a good experience.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:59.100
Yeah. I'm trying to think of a good modern analog to that.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.040
And I know that there are many.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.640
I just can't really think of any.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.500
What's a good modern, terrible game?

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.100
You know, I don't know if it's...

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.220
Studio, sorry. Yeah.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:16.100
Studio. Right. Because I would say that like, you know, nowadays, nothing that I would purchase would

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.280
line up with like data.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:22.240
You know, like we've trained ourselves to know at this age what kind of games we're going

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.400
to like. Honestly, when was the last time you bought a game at full price and said, I hate this?

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.440
You know, it was probably Xbox and PS2 era, I think.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.420
Yeah. Because we were still young enough at that time that like you could be fooled by like a movie

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.680
tie-in game or like, you know, the sports game of the year would fool you into thinking

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.740
like, well, it's another year, so it's going to be better.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.540
And then they actually like remove features.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.040
Mm-hmm. So I think things like that happen.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.000
But I actually can't think about the last time I paid $60 for a game and had to like

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.920
return it. Yeah.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.920
No, I'm on the same page there.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.920
But, you know, but we get to relive it with the data used.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.220
Yeah. Now imagine you paid $60 for Cobra Command and you get to listen to us talk about it for

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.860
an additional 30 to 40 minutes.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.460
That's a, this is a role-playing podcast.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.640
We like to put people in the zone.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.340
Sean, what is Cobra Command?

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:29.520
Cobra Command is a, it's, it's basically a, I guess it's a shmup with adventure elements.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:37.000
It's like, if you just removed all of the context and you were just watching something

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.480
marked as like player character and it would almost look like a, it's just a helicopter

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.760
adventure game, I guess.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.080
Yeah, it does have a feel of a shmup, but actually I, the more I played the game, I

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.200
didn't put it there.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.400
You know, like I thought it was a shmup going in, but it really is more of a action adventure

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.800
game where you're like, you're saving hostages and you're shooting down enemies and yeah,

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:05.040
it's like separated through stages, but it's not so like, it's not like area one, area two.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.400
Like it does, it does have like a seamless nature of storytelling to it.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.460
So I put it more in the action adventure column.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:11.600
I think I'd agree.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.420
And, you know, a helicopter, that's something different.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:20.480
I think like the other helicopter games that we played were like Tiger Heli, which was a,

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:27.800
which was definitely a shmup, but that was a, a vertical shmup where, you know, you, you

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.020
didn't have to worry about, I think you could shoot on land and in the air.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.320
Yes. But you didn't have to worry about like anything other than just making it to the end of the

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.000
stage. You could choose not to shoot anything.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:44.340
Yeah. So we have that, which is a bit more like a, like an expected, like what you would think

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.460
of as a helicopter game.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:51.580
And then we had one of my personal favorites right on bungeling Bay, which was a lot more

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:55.740
technical and kind of abstractly helicopter-y.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.400
So why do you, what do you think about like the appeal of the helicopter?

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.500
Like, is that an appealing thing for you to want to fly?

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.760
Like, does that, is that different than like a spaceship or just a normal fighter jet?

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.080
Like, are you excited by controlling a helicopter?

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.660
I guess like just in the canon of video games, I, I look forward to helicopters.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.520
I, but when I think about that, I just sort of think about like Vice City and Grand Theft

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.960
Auto, like San Andreas, like I get the helicopter.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.340
You can do whatever you want with that.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:35.000
But beyond that, I guess not really, but I guess I don't know what my answer is.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:42.720
Yeah. I just feel like, you know, in, in a war setting, a helicopter is one of the like lesser appealing

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.360
things that I'd want to be flying.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.400
But there's, I mean, it's, it's got novelty to it.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.080
Like it's not a, it's not a plane.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.460
You can, you can go in any direction you want.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.880
I kind of get, I kind of get where they're coming from.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.280
Yeah. I mean, you know, if you think about the fact that you're also rescuing hostages, a helicopter

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.980
makes sense for that kind of mission, you know, you can just like stay stationary above

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.460
the ground and drop a rope and then they come up and then you can, you know, there's

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:10.320
room for them.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.000
I don't think that would be realistic in a fighter jet.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.500
Yeah. You can't really just hang a rope from a fighter jet and hope they pick it up at like Mach

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:19.780
five. Right.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:24.000
I think it was a game though, where we were in like a spaceship that rescued hostages,

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.220
right? Wasn't that, uh, I mean, there's so many of these.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:33.520
I was going to mention that I have, I did not expect there to be this many games in

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:39.380
the NES only a, like less than 200 games in, we've got like three or four games where there's

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:44.240
a rescue hostage mechanic that they just sort of like file in.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:49.800
Like it's such a specific thing to see so often.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:53.880
The 1973 Olympics are like fresh in people's minds at this time.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.580
So I think that's really just like what it came down to.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:02.780
Exactly. You know, I think, I think rescuing hostages was also a big thing in eighties movies.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:07.200
So that would explain, you know, cause video games at this time are leveraging a lot of

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:14.640
the popularity of, you know, that action blockbuster genre that kind of started in 85 and is now

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:15.960
like booming. Yeah.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.600
I can, I can buy that.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:21.840
Yeah. But what do you think about the setting of Indonesia and Thailand?

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:31.400
Thailand. It's a, it's sort of unsettling that they've got such a specific, like usually these games

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:35.840
have been trying to shy away from making them contextual.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.340
Yeah. Political or contextual in the, in the real world at all.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:46.580
Like usually it's like some stand in for the United States against some stand in for the

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:48.840
Soviet, uh, uh, for the USSR.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.880
Uh, but now that they're just going to say like, yeah, you're in Sumatra and Java and

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:54.120
then South China sea.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.800
Um, it's interesting at the very least.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:03.280
I don't think I can list many other games that take place in like Indonesia.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:04.700
No, no.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.140
You know, and I don't know, uh, enough about like this area to know if like there was a

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:11.640
conflict going on or not.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.560
Uh, but it just seems like a, a very specific choice.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.420
Tie that with the helicopter thing.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:21.040
I'm just not sure like American kids were ready for this game.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.860
I guess it's just like, it's close enough to Vietnam while not being Vietnam.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.920
I mean, helicopters are big there too.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.640
This is now, uh, this is now hardcore history.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.420
Uh, right, right. All right.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:40.460
Well, you know, to get away from hardcore history, you are General Steven Power in this

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:41.700
game. It's a great name. Yeah.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.280
Great name, right? Does it matter?

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:47.840
Do you care about like the names of made up characters in these NES games where the story

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.080
is all told in the manual?

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:53.160
This isn't a ridiculous enough name for me to care.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.040
If it's a super ridiculous name, then yes, hell yes.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:01.080
Right. Cause I, I, I read in the manual, you know, just the, the backstory in the Steven Power

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:07.480
thing. And Steven Power first off reminds me of that, uh, when Homer becomes Max Power, uh, that's

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.020
like, that would have been a better pun there.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.080
But, uh, you know, I just don't think like, just say it's me.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.600
Just say like, I have to be in the helicopter doing all this crazy shit.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.320
That, that would make me feel better when I was, if I was a kid during this.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.980
Yeah. So we're talking about the helicopter a lot.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:27.120
Let's talk about how it actually controls in the game because there, there is an interesting

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.740
mechanic of like, you know, it, it tries to simulate how you would be flying a helicopter

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:40.860
in a side scrolling 2d fashion where as you're moving, the helicopter kind of leans forward

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:42.540
or back depending on your momentum.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:48.140
And then that also controls like how you shoot because if the helicopter is leaning, you wouldn't

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:50.340
be able to necessarily shoot a straight shot.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:52.860
What'd you think of that? I kind of liked it.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.760
Uh, it, it made it feel a bit more dynamic and it definitely raises the skill ceiling.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:03.020
Um, because whenever I, I actually don't know.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:07.320
So, uh, yeah, in the, uh, Tiger Heli, I think he just fired straight ahead.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:14.460
And if you dropped a bomb, it would drop like five pixels ahead or something along those

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:21.660
lines. Um, I, I kind of liked that there's a bit more to it than just, uh, it being a one-to-one

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:25.520
relationship to like where your character is on the screen to where you're shooting.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:29.900
It's weird at first, you know, like it takes some getting used to.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:35.060
And to be honest, I actually, I was not a big fan of it at first until I realized that,

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:41.020
you know, if the motions of your helicopter like sway and they have like, uh, you know,

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.980
you have to aim with that in mind, it, you have to like, you have to take the game slow.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.340
You're a helicopter. You're not that fighter jet.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:52.040
So you can't be just like cruising through all the various enemies.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.140
You kind of have to like find your enemy, lock into them and take them down and then

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:58.620
progress as, as you move on.

00:10:58.720 --> 00:10:59.580
Did you find that as well?

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:06.440
Yeah. In the very beginning when I was getting used to it, it felt like, because the way the enemies

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:10.160
spawn in this game, I think it's just based on like your position.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.580
And then if you move back and forth, that enemy will spawn again.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:21.900
Um, but it felt like the timing of enemy spawns made it so that you had to literally crawl

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:22.720
through the level.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:29.080
It was like every, uh, every like quarter second of scroll time, there'd be a new helicopter

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:31.980
that you have to like stop, aim and fire at.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.880
So it kind of like, it's not great for pacing.

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:39.880
Like I think if they, if they switched the, the spawn mechanics up a little bit, it would

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:41.080
feel a little bit more fluid.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:48.560
But yeah, if you're going for this, uh, uh, a bit more like precise and not supposed to

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:52.880
be going at this at a hundred miles an hour, then I guess they did the job.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:58.360
Right. And I, I can't say whether that was actually like an improvement or not, you know, maybe

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:03.580
it's a form of realism, but, uh, you know, to go back to your spawning thing, I found that

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:08.320
to be a major problem because when I was playing the game fast, or even if I was getting tired of

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:12.660
being slow for a minute and just kind of wanted to chug through the next section, I found it

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:15.000
very hard to dodge things in this game.

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:21.560
So it sort of had two different, I had two different feelings, kind of exact opposite

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:26.700
of each other. Uh, yeah, it's hard to do that, but there are also sections in this game where you could

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:30.940
just ignore, you could just ignore enemies because enemies don't really linger.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:36.340
If, if you're, if you're going towards a, uh, another helicopter, they're just going

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:41.880
to fly past you and it's not until the later levels that you get ones that are, uh, that

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.400
are sort of trying to get in your way more.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:50.120
So in some instances it could feel way too easy if you just ignore everything, like in

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:51.740
a lot of games that we've played.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:56.880
Uh, but, uh, yeah, it's just an uneven difficulty curve, I'd say.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:03.620
Yeah. I always feel like a game that, um, that you can avoid, uh, enemies, even though that might

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:05.920
be helpful to like a player who needs a break.

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:11.340
I always see that as just a bad sign for a game in general, because it's kind of lazy

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:16.220
game design philosophy to make it so that enemies don't necessarily matter.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:19.740
And they're just pre-programmed to spawn in specific spots, you know?

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:28.160
Yeah. You're just sort of, uh, banking on the, uh, the behaviors that people that play games

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:30.000
do, which is like, oh, I have to fight this thing.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.600
So I will, and not really realizing that like, oh, I could just go past them.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:42.780
Right. And, and, you know, when you get damage, uh, to your helicopter, it's, it's, first off,

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.880
it's nice that it's not just a one shot kill.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:49.940
We're so used to that in games, but the helicopter will actually gradually start to catch on fire

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:53.480
and slowly descend if it's left alone.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:57.120
I've never seen something like that before in one of these games.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:01.020
And I thought it was so cool that they, uh, that they decided to do that.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:02.480
I like that a lot too.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:08.760
Like the, there's a, I think there's like two stages to, uh, like visible damage.

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:12.720
Like you can take a couple of shots and you'll still look the same, but then on the first

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:17.760
one, if you just leave your controller alone, you will very slowly start to descend and you

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:24.780
can kind of see fire or smoke and then another shot will make it so that no matter how hard

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:29.300
you fight, you're, you're still going, you're like, you're very slowly crashing.

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:30.860
And then the last one will blow you up.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:37.920
Um, I, I really liked that too, just for, um, just for the, uh, immersion of it.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:42.740
Um, yeah, there's like a sense of dread in the, in that final one, because, you know, it

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:46.620
is interesting that you're able to control while still being on fire and you feel like this

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:50.860
pushback, but I think it's exactly when you hit like zero health.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.980
So you're technically already dead, but it lets you like, it lets that sink in.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:57.420
Yeah, for sure.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:01.440
And I, I don't know how other games would have applied that so far.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:06.160
It's, it would be weird with like a, uh, a human protagonist to do something like that

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:10.040
where they start like stumbling and then they fall to the ground or something like that

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:13.580
would be, uh, maybe too creepy for an NES game.

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:16.540
Yeah. But it's done really nicely here.

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:20.120
And that kind of makes me wish that it also happened to enemies.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:25.020
Oh yeah. Um, yeah, that, that does make sense.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:26.440
They just kind of vanish, right?

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:28.500
They almost like evaporate when you hit them.

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:30.980
Yeah. I don't even have like an explosion or anything.

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:35.680
Yeah. It's all, it almost feels inconsequential whenever you do something to an enemy.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:36.900
It's just like, Oh, now it's gone.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:43.040
Um, but it's only because of the context of it happening to you that I guess it feels like

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:50.320
that though. You can fire both, um, I guess like regular bullets and then, uh, missiles as well, uh,

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:52.080
between A and B.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:54.400
Uh, you know, that's pretty basic.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:58.560
And I like that you can fire them at the same time because I find myself just like hitting

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:00.040
both across my thumb.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:04.160
Like I just hold my thumb sideways instead and just spam it that way.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:06.060
